
;;
	the stack is the main working area of a socks program
	values are pushed onto it and operations consume the
	values they need and push back their results

	each value on the stack takes up a slot of the size of
	its type, aligned to eight bytes internally, but the
	program only sees values in terms of their declared type

	integer literals push their value
	operators like + - * / consume two values and push one
	built-in words like dmp consume one value and push nothing

	the stack must be balanced at every scope boundary:
	the net effect of a procedure body, branch body, or loop
	body must match the declared signature or the required
	shape of the enclosing construct
;;

main:(){
    1 2 + dmp
}